\title{Constructive Integration in Finite Terms\\for a Rational--Exponential Normal Form}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{3 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a constructive integration algorithm for a finite normal form consisting of polynomial monomials, simple rational poles, higher-order rational poles, and constant-rate exponential terms, all with rational parameters. The algorithm separates the input into algebraic, logarithmic, Hermite-reduced rational, and hyperexponential stages and returns an explicit expression assembled from arithmetic, logarithms, and exponentials. We prove local analytic soundness at every point outside the listed poles, closure of both input and output in the elementary expression language, and completeness relative to the stated grammar. A zero-rate branch handles constant exponential terms without partiality. For rational functions already supplied in explicit partial-fraction form, one abstract step is charged per summand; the resulting cost is linear in a natural weighted representation size and hence polynomial. The result isolates a rigorous terminal stage of the Risch program while leaving arbitrary-expression normalization and bit complexity as separate questions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Symbolic integration asks whether a function has an antiderivative expressible in a prescribed finite language and, if so, asks for such an expression. This differs from numerical quadrature, which approximates a definite integral. The Risch approach recasts elementary integration in differential algebra, reducing expressions through algebraic, logarithmic, and exponential extensions until algebraic tests and differential equations decide elementary integrability.

We study a concrete terminal normal form in which reduction has already exposed every summand as a polynomial monomial $cx^n$, a simple pole $r/(x-a)$, a higher pole $d/(x-b)^m$ with $m\geq2$, or a constant-rate exponential $ue^{vx}$. All scalar data are rational. We define a total four-stage procedure, prove its derivative identity on the regular domain, prove closure in a finite elementary language, and establish a linear step bound for normalized rational input. Completeness is relative to this grammar; arbitrary-expression normalization and global complexity are not assumed.

\section{Language and analytic interpretation}

\begin{definition}[Elementary expression language]
Let $\mathcal E$ be the smallest class of real expressions generated by real constants, the variable $x$, addition, multiplication, multiplicative inversion, logarithm, and exponential. Natural powers and rational scalar multiplication are abbreviations. A function represented by such an expression on its domain is called \emph{finitely elementary}.
\end{definition}

Each expression denotes a real function wherever inverses and real logarithms are defined. For a simple pole we write the primitive as $\log|x-a|$; on either connected component of $\mathbb R\setminus\{a\}$ its derivative is $1/(x-a)$. If one chooses $\log(x-a)$ instead, the derivative statement is restricted to $x>a$.

For $P\in\mathcal E$ define $P^0=1$ and $P^{n+1}=P^nP$. For $a,c\in\mathbb Q$, put $S_a(x)=x-a$ and let $cP$ denote rational scalar multiplication.

\begin{lemma}[Evaluation of abbreviations]
At every point where $P$ is defined,
\[
(P(x))^n=P(x)^n,\qquad S_a(x)=x-a,\qquad (cP)(x)=cP(x).
\]
Finite expression sums evaluate as ordinary finite sums.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The power identity follows by induction on $n$. The remaining identities follow from the semantics of constants, addition, multiplication, and the recursively defined empty and nonempty sums.
\end{proof}

\section{Normalized inputs}

\begin{definition}[Pieces]
An \emph{algebraic piece} $(c,n)\in\mathbb Q\times\mathbb N$ represents $cx^n$. A \emph{logarithmic piece} $(r,a)\in\mathbb Q^2$ represents $r/(x-a)$. A \emph{higher-pole piece} $(d,b,m)$, where $d,b\in\mathbb Q$ and $m\geq2$, represents $d/(x-b)^m$. An \emph{exponential piece} $(u,v)\in\mathbb Q^2$ represents $ue^{vx}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rational--exponential normal form]
A normal form is a quadruple $R=(A,L,H,E)$ of finite lists of the four kinds of pieces. Its integrand is
\begin{align*}
f_R(x)={}&\sum_{(c,n)\in A}cx^n
+\sum_{(r,a)\in L}\frac{r}{x-a}\\
&+\sum_{(d,b,m)\in H}\frac{d}{(x-b)^m}
+\sum_{(u,v)\in E}ue^{vx}.
\end{align*}
A point $x$ is \emph{regular} if $x\ne a$ for every $(r,a)\in L$ and $x\ne b$ for every $(d,b,m)\in H$.
\end{definition}

The lower bound $m\geq2$ separates simple poles, whose primitives are logarithmic, from repeated poles, which integrate rationally. Polynomial and exponential terms have no real singularities, so the regularity condition lists exactly the poles.

\section{The four-stage algorithm}

The piecewise rules are
\[
cx^n\longmapsto \frac{c}{n+1}x^{n+1},\qquad
\frac r{x-a}\longmapsto r\log|x-a|,
\]
\[
\frac d{(x-b)^m}\longmapsto
-\frac d{m-1}(x-b)^{-(m-1)},
\]
and
\[
ue^{vx}\longmapsto
\begin{cases}
(u/v)e^{vx},&v\ne0,\\
ux,&v=0.
\end{cases}
\]
The zero-rate branch is essential: $e^{0x}=1$, whereas division by $v$ is undefined at zero.

\begin{algorithm}[Four-stage normalized integration]
Given $R=(A,L,H,E)$, traverse each list once. Replace every algebraic, logarithmic, and higher-pole piece by the corresponding primitive above. For $(u,v)\in E$, test $v=0$ and select the appropriate exponential branch. Return the sum of all generated expressions.
\end{algorithm}

Explicitly, the output is
\begin{align*}
F_R(x)={}&\sum_{(c,n)\in A}\frac{c}{n+1}x^{n+1}
+\sum_{(r,a)\in L}r\log|x-a|\\
&-\sum_{(d,b,m)\in H}\frac{d}{m-1}(x-b)^{-(m-1)}\\
&+\sum_{\substack{(u,v)\in E\\v\ne0}}\frac uv e^{vx}
+\sum_{(u,0)\in E}ux.
\end{align*}
All lists are finite and equality in $\mathbb Q$ is decidable, so the procedure terminates and every branch is effective.

\section{Correctness of individual stages}

\begin{lemma}[Algebraic stage]
For $c\in\mathbb Q$, $n\in\mathbb N$, and $x\in\mathbb R$,
\[
\frac d{dx}\left(\frac c{n+1}x^{n+1}\right)=cx^n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The power rule produces $(n+1)x^n$, and multiplication by $c/(n+1)$ cancels the positive integer $n+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Simple-pole stage]
For $r,a\in\mathbb Q$ and $x\ne a$,
\[
\frac d{dx}\bigl(r\log|x-a|\bigr)=\frac r{x-a}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
On either component of $\mathbb R\setminus\{a\}$, the chain rule gives $(\log|x-a|)'=(x-a)^{-1}$. Multiply by $r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Higher-pole stage]
For $d,b\in\mathbb Q$, $m\geq2$, and $x\ne b$,
\[
\frac d{dx}\left(-\frac d{m-1}(x-b)^{-(m-1)}\right)
=\frac d{(x-b)^m}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Set $k=m-1\geq1$. The chain and power rules give $((x-b)^{-k})'=-k(x-b)^{-(k+1)}$. Multiplication by $-d/k$ yields the claim. The non-pole hypothesis justifies inversion.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exponential stage]
For $u,v\in\mathbb Q$, define
\[
P_{u,v}(x)=\begin{cases}(u/v)e^{vx},&v\ne0,\\ux,&v=0.\end{cases}
\]
Then $P_{u,v}'(x)=ue^{vx}$ for every real $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $v\ne0$, the chain rule gives $(e^{vx})'=ve^{vx}$ and the factors $v$ cancel. For $v=0$, the integrand is the constant $u$ and $(ux)'=u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finite summation]
If $P_i'(x)=f_i(x)$ for $1\leq i\leq q$, then
\[
\left(\sum_{i=1}^qP_i\right)'(x)=\sum_{i=1}^qf_i(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $q$, using the derivative of the zero function and linearity of differentiation.
\end{proof}

\section{Main theorems}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of normalized integration]
Let $R$ be a rational--exponential normal form and let $x$ be regular for $R$. The output $F_R$ is differentiable at $x$ and
\[
F_R'(x)=f_R(x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the four stage lemmas to every piece. Regularity supplies all required denominator nonvanishing conditions. Apply finite summation within each stage and then across the four stages.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-language closure]
For every normal form $R$, both $f_R$ and $F_R$ are finitely elementary.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each input and output piece is assembled from rational constants, the variable, arithmetic, inversion, logarithm, and exponential. The language is closed under finite sums.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive finite-term integration]
For every rational--exponential normal form $R$, there exists a finitely elementary expression $F$ such that for every regular point $x$,
\[
F'(x)=f_R(x).
\]
The four-stage algorithm constructs such an $F$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $F=F_R$. Finite-language closure gives representability, and soundness gives the derivative identity. Construction and termination are explicit in the algorithm.
\end{proof}

This theorem is complete relative to the stated grammar because every admitted constructor has a branch. It does not assert that arbitrary elementary expressions have already been normalized or that all such expressions possess elementary primitives.

\section{Rational specialization and complexity}

A rational partial-fraction normal form consists only of polynomial, simple-pole, and higher-pole lists. Let their lengths be $P,S,H$. Charge one abstract step per summand and put
\[
T=P+S+H,\qquad N=1+2P+3S+4H.
\]
The additive one accounts for the outer constructor, while the weights conservatively charge for the fields of each record.

\begin{theorem}[Linear and polynomial bounds]
For every rational partial-fraction normal form,
\[
T\leq N\qquad\text{and}\qquad T\leq N^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity gives $P+S+H\leq1+2P+3S+4H=N$. Since $N\geq1$, one also has $N\leq N^2$, and transitivity proves the second inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Termination for normalized rational input]
The terminal integration pass takes a number of summand-processing steps linear in $N$, and therefore polynomial in $N$.
\end{corollary}

This representation-level cost does not include conversion of an arbitrary quotient into partial fractions, arithmetic bit costs, factorization, or coefficient growth. Those are separate normalization questions.

\section{Example and numerical illustration}

Consider
\[
f(x)=3x^2-\frac2{x-1}+\frac5{(x+2)^3}+4e^{2x}+7e^{0x}.
\]
The algorithm returns
\[
F(x)=x^3-2\log|x-1|-\frac5{2(x+2)^2}+2e^{2x}+7x.
\]
At every $x\ne1,-2$,
\begin{align*}
F'(x)
&=3x^2-\frac2{x-1}+5(x+2)^{-3}+4e^{2x}+7\\
&=f(x).
\end{align*}
For illustration one can compare $f(x)$ with the centered difference
\[
D_hF(x)=\frac{F(x+h)-F(x-h)}{2h}.
\]
Away from poles this has second-order truncation error for smooth $F$. Near poles numerical conditioning deteriorates, although the exact local identity remains valid at every non-pole point.

\section{Applications, scope, and limitations}

The normal form is an interpretable intermediate representation: each primitive term traces to one input record. It exposes singular structure, makes simple-pole residues the coefficients of logarithms, and lowers repeated pole orders rationally. Exact rational parameters avoid ambiguous equality tests. The same decomposition is useful in differential equations, where rational forcing and constant-rate exponentials arise naturally.

The residue interpretation also connects the construction to complex analysis. For a rational function with split denominator, the coefficient of $(x-a)^{-1}$ determines the logarithmic contribution. Higher principal-part terms integrate rationally; the residue records the obstruction to a purely rational primitive.

The theorem starts after normalization. It neither computes a canonical partial-fraction decomposition nor solves the general differential equations arising from products $R(x)e^{ax}$. In that broader setting one seeks a rational $Q$ satisfying
\[
Q'(x)+aQ(x)=R(x),
\]
since $(Qe^{ax})'=(Q'+aQ)e^{ax}$. Constant $R$ is the case handled here. A general integrability decision must also represent negative outcomes when no elementary primitive exists.

\section{Future directions}

Five extensions are natural:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Construct canonical partial-fraction normalization for every rational function over $\mathbb Q$, unique up to permutation and removal of zero terms.
\item Prove a polynomial bit-complexity bound in degree and coefficient bit length for squarefree factorization and Hermite reduction.
\item Establish Liouville completeness for one exponential extension $\mathbb Q(x,e^{ax})$ by characterizing elementary integrability through rational solutions of the associated first-order differential equation.
\item Prove the residue criterion for rational denominators split into rational linear factors, identifying logarithmic coefficients with simple-pole residues.
\item Build semantics-preserving general normalization that strictly decreases a well-founded tower-complexity measure at each recursive reduction.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For the rational--exponential normal form, integration is a finite list transformation governed by four derivative identities. Polynomial powers rise by one, simple poles become logarithms, repeated poles fall by one, and exponentials divide by their rate, with a necessary linear branch at rate zero. The generated expression differentiates to the input at every regular point and remains in a finite elementary language. For normalized rational partial fractions, the terminal pass is linear in a natural weighted representation size. Keeping this result separate from normalization and bit complexity gives an exact account of both its strength and its boundary.

\end{document}

